</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We reduced greatly the error and empirically proved the correctness of our emulated float implementation against GNU’s.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS3.4" class="ltx_text">V-C</span> </span><span id="S5.SS3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Softmax</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One of the steps needed to compute the self-attention is the evaluation of the <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">softmax</span> function, the original implementation, described in Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ V-C Softmax ‣ V Floating-point arithmetic ‣ Microscaling Floating Point Formatsfor Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, firstly computes and stores the exponential function for all elements while computing the sum of the resulting values, then it normalizes by the sum by re-scanning the values once more.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> The attention’s softmax</figcaption>
<div id="alg2.3" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg2.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              
            <span id="alg2.l1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> array <math id="alg2.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> of size <math id="alg2.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, maval the maximum of <math id="alg2.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              
            <span id="alg2.l2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output:</span> normalized array

</div>
<div id="alg2.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              
            <math id="alg2.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\leftarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\leftarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg2.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Calculate the exp and keep track of sum
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              
            <span id="alg2.l4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg2.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leftarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leftarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg2.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              <math id="alg2.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\leftarrow\exp(a[i]-\text{maxval})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mtext>maxval</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\leftarrow\exp(a[i]-\text{maxval})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              <math id="alg2.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\leftarrow s+e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\leftarrow s+e</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              <math id="alg2.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v[i]\leftarrow e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v[i]\leftarrow e</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              
            <span id="alg2.l8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg2.l8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              
            <math id="alg2.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\prime}\leftarrow\left\{\begin{array}[]{ll}0&amp;\text{if }s=0\\
\dfrac{1}{s}&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{array}\right." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>s</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\prime}\leftarrow\left\{\begin{array}[]{ll}0&amp;\text{if }s=0\\
\dfrac{1}{s}&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{array}\right.</annotation></semantics></math>
<span id="alg2.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg2.l9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Normalize to get the softmax
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              
            <span id="alg2.l10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg2.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leftarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leftarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg2.l10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              <math id="alg2.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v[i]\leftarrow v[i]\cdot s^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v[i]\leftarrow v[i]\cdot s^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              
            <span id="alg2.l12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg2.l12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">When using full-precision such an operation presents no drawbacks as their dynamic range is more than sufficient to represent the largest value of the exponential function one might encounter in this kind of application; however when using formats with a reduced dynamic range, such as the eight bit formats used in Microscaling, many of the results of the exponential function will undoubtedly cause an overflow causing an erroneous computation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To solve this issue, we modified the implementation of the attention’s softmax to iterate two times over the values, one to compute the exponential function on-the-fly — that is without storing the result in memory — just to accumulate all its values into an accumulator, and a second one to compute the exponential function divided by the total sum.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">While the computational complexity remains in the order of <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta\left(t\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta\left(t\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, the machine now has to compute two times the same exponential function; this might negatively affect larger models, even though the matrix multiplication remains — without any doubt — the biggest bottleneck in increasing the size of these models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS4.4" class="ltx_text">V-D</span> </span><span id="S5.SS4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Look-up tables</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To speed-up such operations, we added optional support for look-up tables (LUTs) to the Anyfloat class. When enabled, it replaces certain operations with one or two reads to the appropriate look-up tables, thus greatly reducing the number of CPU cycles required to perform the computation. We also modified the binary operators to perform these operations in a different output space, instead of being a closed operation, typically with higher precision — e.g., binary operations in E5M2 promote to brain float 16 bit — as this is necessary to perform the dot product and it is where most of the these heavy operations are performed; if need be, the result can be converted back to the original space, in such scenario we still perform less conversions, originally three conversions were performed and now only one.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S5.SS4.SSS1.4" class="ltx_text">V-D</span>1 </span>Constant expression</h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While it is technically possible to compute and store the look-up tables as <span id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">constexpr</span> objects, there are two main drawbacks in doing so:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">while populating the tables we would have to handle operations that generate <span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">NaN</span>s or exceptions — such as <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\cdot\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\cdot\infty</annotation></semantics></math> — separately as it is not possible to compute them at compilation time, as their behavior depends on runtime variables
(The compiler throws an error if said operations are encountered in a <span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">constexpr</span>)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the compiler would have to create the same tables multiple times, a copy for each compilation unit, possibly hindering the compilation speed.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">we decided to keep the tables as a static constant member to be built during the process start-up. One downside of our approach is a very likely <span id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">static initialization order fiasco</span> when defining other static variables that depend on the look-up tables being already populated; however, such a scenario is not present in our project.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S5.SS4.SSS2.4" class="ltx_text">V-D</span>2 </span>On the complexity of operations</h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let us call <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> the number of bits used to represent a number used as input operand and <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> the number of bits used to represent the computation resulting number and <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{M}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕄</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{M}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{M}^{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕄</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{M}^{o}</annotation></semantics></math>
the space of floating-point numbers used as input and output respectively. Contrary to the common practise — that is <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\bowtie\texttt{NaN}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⋈</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">NaN</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\bowtie\texttt{NaN}</annotation></semantics></math> is always false with any binary operator <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bowtie\;=\;&lt;,\leq,\geq,&gt;,\dots\;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⋈</mo><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo rspace="0em">&lt;</mo><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">≤</mo><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">≥</mo><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">&gt;</mo><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bowtie\;=\;&lt;,\leq,\geq,&gt;,\dots\;</annotation></semantics></math> — we consider all positive <span id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">NaN</span>s part of <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{M}^{i}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝕄</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{M}^{i}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> and all negative <span id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">NaN</span>s part of <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{M}^{i}_{\leq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝕄</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{M}^{i}_{\leq 0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We introduced the following tables:</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Inversion table</h5>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It defines a function <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{L}_{i}:\mathbb{M}^{i}_{\geq 0}\rightarrow\mathbb{M}^{i}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐋</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝕄</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>𝕄</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{L}_{i}:\mathbb{M}^{i}_{\geq 0}\rightarrow\mathbb{M}^{i}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math>, that is used to quickly compute the reciprocal <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>x</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. We do not need to store all the input space as the function is odd in its domain. Its space complexity is</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S5.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathbf{L}_{i}}\in\Theta\left(b_{i}\cdot 2^{b_{i}}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><msub><mi>𝐋</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathbf{L}_{i}}\in\Theta\left(b_{i}\cdot 2^{b_{i}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">for a 8 bit number, such as the E5M2 format, the space occupation the table is <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{7}*1\texttt{byte}=128\texttt{byte}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>7</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">byte</mtext></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>128</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">byte</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{7}*1\texttt{byte}=128\texttt{byte}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Multiplication table</h5>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It defines a function
<math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{L}_{m}:\mathbb{M}^{i}_{\geq 0}\times\mathbb{M}^{i}_{\geq 0}\rightarrow\mathbb{M}^{o}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐋</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝕄</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>𝕄</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>𝕄</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>o</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{L}_{m}:\mathbb{M}^{i}_{\geq 0}\times\mathbb{M}^{i}_{\geq 0}\rightarrow\mathbb{M}^{o}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math>, that is used to compute
<math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=x\cdot y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=x\cdot y</annotation></semantics></math>. We don’t have to store all the input space as the function is even when <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{sign}(x)=\text{sign}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>sign</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>sign</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{sign}(x)=\text{sign}(y)</annotation></semantics></math> and odd otherwise, so we perform the look-up using the absolute values and set the sign-bit accordingly. Its space complexity is</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S5.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathbf{L}_{m}}\in\Theta\left(b_{o}\cdot 2^{b_{i}}\cdot 2^{b_{i}}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><msub><mi>𝐋</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathbf{L}_{m}}\in\Theta\left(b_{o}\cdot 2^{b_{i}}\cdot 2^{b_{i}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">using the brain-float16 data format for the output, for an 8 bit number, the space occupation is
<math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{7}\cdot 2^{7}\cdot 2\texttt{byte}=32\texttt{kibibyte}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>7</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>7</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">byte</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">kibibyte</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{7}\cdot 2^{7}\cdot 2\texttt{byte}=32\texttt{kibibyte}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We could further reduce the space occupation by just storing the triangular matrix of the original look-up table and performing the look-up by imposing <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\geq y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\geq y</annotation></semantics></math>, under this assumption the space occupation could be reduced to <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{2^{7}}{2}\cdot 2\texttt{byte}=15.875\texttt{kibibyte}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>7</mn></msup><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">byte</mtext></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>15.875</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">kibibyte</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{2^{7}}{2}\cdot 2\texttt{byte}=15.875\texttt{kibibyte}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01863v1/lut_mul.svg" id="S5.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/339;" width="476" height="339"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 6: </span>How a multiplication is performed using a look-up table</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Addition table</h5>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It defines a function
<math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{L}_{a}:\mathbb{M}^{i}\times\mathbb{M}^{i}_{\geq 0}\rightarrow\mathbb{M}^{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐋</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝕄</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>𝕄</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝕄</mi><mi>o</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{L}_{a}:\mathbb{M}^{i}\times\mathbb{M}^{i}_{\geq 0}\rightarrow\mathbb{M}^{o}</annotation></semantics></math>, that is used to compute
<math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=a+b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=a+b</annotation></semantics></math>. To save space we don’t save the negative values for one addendum, that is we can look-up the result of an addition only if at least one of the operands is not negative; it is quite easy to see that we can still compute the addition for all possible values with the relation:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S5.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a+b=\begin{cases}-(-a\oplus(-b))&amp;\text{if}\;a&lt;0\land b&lt;0\\
b\oplus a&amp;\text{if}\;a\geq 0\land b&lt;0\\
a\oplus b&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if</mtext><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∧</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if</mtext><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∧</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a+b=\begin{cases}-(-a\oplus(-b))&amp;\text{if}\;a&lt;0\land b&lt;0\\
b\oplus a&amp;\text{if}\;a\geq 0\land b&lt;0\\
a\oplus b&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\oplus y=\mathbf{L}_{a}\left(x,y\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐋</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\oplus y=\mathbf{L}_{a}\left(x,y\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, with such an approach the space requirement of the table is in the order of</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S5.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathbf{L}_{a}}\in\Theta\left(b_{o}\cdot 2^{b_{i}}\cdot 2^{b_{i}}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><msub><mi>𝐋</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathbf{L}_{a}}\in\Theta\left(b_{o}\cdot 2^{b_{i}}\cdot 2^{b_{i}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">using the brain-float16 data format for the output, for an 8 bit number, the space occupation is
<math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{8}\cdot 2^{7}\cdot 2\texttt{byte}=64\texttt{kibibyte}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>8</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>7</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">byte</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">kibibyte</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{8}\cdot 2^{7}\cdot 2\texttt{byte}=64\texttt{kibibyte}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Other operations</h5>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With similar reasoning as with multiplication table, we could further reduce the space occupation by dividing the table in two:
<math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{L}_{a}^{+}:\mathbb{M}^{i}_{\geq 0}\times\mathbb{M}^{i}_{\geq 0}\rightarrow\mathbb{M}^{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐋</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝕄</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>𝕄</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝕄</mi><mi>o</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{L}_{a}^{+}:\mathbb{M}^{i}_{\geq 0}\times\mathbb{M}^{i}_{\geq 0}\rightarrow\mathbb{M}^{o}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{L}_{a}^{-}:\mathbb{M}^{i}_{\leq 0}\times\mathbb{M}^{i}_{\geq 0}\rightarrow\mathbb{M}^{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐋</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝕄</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>𝕄</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝕄</mi><mi>o</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{L}_{a}^{-}:\mathbb{M}^{i}_{\leq 0}\times\mathbb{M}^{i}_{\geq 0}\rightarrow\mathbb{M}^{o}</annotation></semantics></math> —
that is one for positive sums and other for negative sums (subtractions), — then store only the diagonal matrix of each one by imposing
<math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|x|\geq|y|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|x|\geq|y|</annotation></semantics></math>, thus the space occupation would be reduced to <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\cdot\binom{2^{7}}{2\,}\cdot 2\,\texttt{byte}=31.75\,\texttt{kibibyte}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>7</mn></msup><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">byte</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>31.75</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">kibibyte</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\cdot\binom{2^{7}}{2\,}\cdot 2\,\texttt{byte}=31.75\,\texttt{kibibyte}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Other operations</h5>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With such tables it is also possible to perform the following operations:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.SSS2.Px5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S5.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">subtraction as <math id="S5.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a+(-b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a+(-b)</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">division as <math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\cdot\frac{1}{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>b</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\cdot\frac{1}{b}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">conversion to a higher precision format, assuming the LUT is setup to perform operations with promotion, by performing a look-up to
<math id="S5.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a+0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a+0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S5.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\cdot 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\cdot 1</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS5.4" class="ltx_text">V-E</span> </span><span id="S5.SS5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Exact accumulator</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When computing the dot-product as described in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="III-B The Microscaling data-format ‣ III Microscaling Floating-Point Formats ‣ Microscaling Floating Point Formatsfor Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-B</span></a>, it is necessary to use a numerical format with higher dynamic precision to represent the intra-block accumulator, this is especially important when using Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Chunk construction ‣ III-C Microscaling implementation ‣ III Microscaling Floating-Point Formats ‣ Microscaling Floating Point Formatsfor Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> to quantize the values; otherwise, if an FP accumulator with the same dynamic range as <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{M}_{RP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{M}_{RP}</annotation></semantics></math> is used, an overflow is most likely to occur.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Such a statement is quite easy to prove, let us assume that <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RP=\text{E5M2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext>E5M2</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RP=\text{E5M2}</annotation></semantics></math>, we know from how the block is built that <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exists i\quad c_{i}=m_{i}\cdot 2^{8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>8</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exists i\quad c_{i}=m_{i}\cdot 2^{8}</annotation></semantics></math>, let us say that there are two consecutive elements that satisfy the predicate, <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{sign}\left(m_{0}\right)=\text{sign}\left(m_{1}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>sign</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>sign</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{sign}\left(m_{0}\right)=\text{sign}\left(m_{1}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, then the computer will have to compute the following expressions:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S5.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle a</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leftarrow 0.00" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>0.00</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leftarrow 0.00</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle a</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leftarrow a\oplus m_{0}\cdot 2^{8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>8</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leftarrow a\oplus m_{0}\cdot 2^{8}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle a</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leftarrow a\oplus m_{1}\cdot 2^{8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>8</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leftarrow a\oplus m_{1}\cdot 2^{8}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is a E5M2 accumulator, as <math id="S5.SS5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> cannot store a number with exponent <math id="S5.SS5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math> and an overflow occurs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">With software emulation we can use full-precision accumulators and avoid this issue completely; however a possible hardware implementation or even a software approach based on look-up tables may find the use of higher precision accumulators slower or more expensive to implement, so we propose a simple architecture to compute the dot-product that does not require the use of expensive floating-point circuitry.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our approach works with all kind of data; however it might be possible to build a cheaper and faster architecture by exploiting possible statistical distributions that are commonly found in values stored in deep neural networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F7" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01863v1/acc_full_prec_e4m3.svg" id="S5.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/529;" width="476" height="529"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 7: </span>Simplified implementation of a circuit that computes the dot products between two E4M3 Microscaling vectors without rounding error. The logic to handle special cases, such as overflows, <span id="S5.F7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">NaN</span>s, infinities etc…; has been omitted for simplicity’s sake. The multi-adder could be implemented as a <math id="S5.F7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lceil\log_{2}\left(B\right)\right\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>⌈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⌉</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lceil\log_{2}\left(B\right)\right\rceil</annotation></semantics></math> levels tall tree of adders.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Multiplication</h5>

<div id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let us consider the multiplication between two normalized floating point numbers, whose mantissae and exponents are represented on M and E bits respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S5.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x=-1^{s_{x}}\cdot m_{x}\cdot 2^{\xi_{x}}\qquad y=-1^{s_{y}}\cdot m_{y}\cdot 2^{\xi_{y}}\qquad m_{x},m_{y}\in\left[1,2\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msup><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msup><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=-1^{s_{x}}\cdot m_{x}\cdot 2^{\xi_{x}}\qquad y=-1^{s_{y}}\cdot m_{y}\cdot 2^{\xi_{y}}\qquad m_{x},m_{y}\in\left[1,2\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The product <math id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=x\cdot y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=x\cdot y</annotation></semantics></math> will have its mantissa <math id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{z}=m_{x}\cdot m_{y}\in\left[1,4\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{z}=m_{x}\cdot m_{y}\in\left[1,4\right)</annotation></semantics></math> represented on <math id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2E+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2E+2</annotation></semantics></math> bits and its exponent will be <math id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{x}+\xi_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{x}+\xi_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>; if the mantissa falls in the range <math id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left[2,4\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left[2,4\right)</annotation></semantics></math> it needs to be normalized, this is done by dividing the mantissa by two and by increasing the exponent by one. The exponent is stored on <math id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E+2</annotation></semantics></math> bits <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, Section 11.2]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Thus the resulting significand can be stored, without rounding, on <math id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2M+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2M+1</annotation></semantics></math> bits, or it can be rounded and stored in a smaller format. With similar reasoning, the exponent can be stored on <math id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E+2</annotation></semantics></math> bits or it can be stored in a smaller format by handling appropriately the possible overflow.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Addition</h5>

<div id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is possible to completely rule out such an issue by performing the addition in a fixed-point format large enough to represent all the possible mantissae and their magnitude at once; that is an integral number in which the significand associated to the number of smallest magnitude occupies the <math id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> least significative bits of the number, whereas the one associated to the largest magnitude occupies the <math id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> most significative bits of the number. Negative addenda are represented in the two’s complement.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It is easy to prove that for a floating-point format with an exponent range of <math id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi\in\left[\xi_{\text{min}},\xi_{\text{max}}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi\in\left[\xi_{\text{min}},\xi_{\text{max}}\right]</annotation></semantics></math> and a mantissa represented on <math id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> bits, the fixed-pointed format must be <math id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{\text{max}}-\xi_{\text{min}}+1+E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{\text{max}}-\xi_{\text{min}}+1+E</annotation></semantics></math> bits wide. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, Chapter 21.1]</cite></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The sum is then performed using integral adders and final result can then be converted back to a floating-point format.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ Addition ‣ V-E Exact accumulator ‣ V Floating-point arithmetic ‣ Microscaling Floating Point Formatsfor Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a> shows a comparison of the floating-point width necessary to accumulate the products of numbers represented in various input formats.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE II: </span>Format required to represent an exact multiplication</figcaption>
<table id="S5.T2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Operand type</span></th>
<th id="S5.T2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" colspan="2"><span id="S5.T2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Operand’s limits</span></th>
<th id="S5.T2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" colspan="2"><span id="S5.T2.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Product’s limits</span></th>
<th id="S5.T2.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T2.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Product type</span></th>
<th id="S5.T2.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T2.1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">fixed-point</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td"></td>
<th id="S5.T2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column"><math id="S5.T2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{\text{max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{\text{max}}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S5.T2.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column"><math id="S5.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{\text{min}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{\text{min}}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S5.T2.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column"><math id="S5.T2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{\text{max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{\text{max}}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S5.T2.1.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column"><math id="S5.T2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{\text{min}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{\text{min}}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S5.T2.1.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_column"></th>
