</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Implementation</h5>

<div id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We added a new template class called <span id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">exact_accumulator</span> that implements a software-defined exact accumulator, whose parameters are the minimum exponent representable and the mantissa width in bits. For simplicity’s sake we programmed the accumulator to be always <span id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">int64_t</span> — that is a 64 integral number, — this restrict the implementation to work only with smaller formats; we also don’t handle any corner case, like overflows, infinties etc… as we are certain they do not manifest themselves in our use-case.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The class exposes two public methods, the <span id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">operator+=()</span> used to add a value to the accumulator and the <span id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">unpack()</span> used to “unpack” the fixed point accumulator into a Unpacked data-structure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.F7" title="Fig. 7 ‣ V-E Exact accumulator ‣ V Floating-point arithmetic ‣ Microscaling Floating Point Formatsfor Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> shows how a possible hardware implementation of such a Microscaling dot-product with exact accumulators could be implemented.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VI </span><span id="S6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Evaluation and Results</span></h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We performed two kinds of tests to gauge the performance of our Microscaling implementation, firstly we fine-tuned a GPT2 model with 124 million parameters on the Tiny Shakespeare corpus and compared the learning trend with the base implementation; then we ran an inferential test on a GPT2 model with 1.6 billion parameters. Both models were originally trained by Karpathy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We tested a <span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">quantization</span> approach, where an already trained model — originally trained in full or half precision — stores some or all of its numerical values in a lower precision than the format used during training.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">VI-A</span> </span><span id="S6.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fine-tuning</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Due to the expensiveness of training a large langue model on the CPU instead of leveraging the GPU’s capabilities, as well as the massive overhead introduced by the introduction of software-defined numerical formats; it was very difficult to train the network for a significative number of iterations and we had to settle to one hundred iterations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S6.F8" title="Fig. 8 ‣ VI-B Non-standard 8-bits floating-point formats ‣ VI Evaluation and Results ‣ Microscaling Floating Point Formatsfor Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> shows the various learning curves for various tested configurations:</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S6.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Line <span id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baseline</span> shows the learning curve of the network ran with the default settings, that is with vectors of full-precision floats</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Line <span id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A</span> shows the learning curve of the network while using a normal vector of bfloat16 values</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Line <span id="S6.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">B</span> is the same as <span id="S6.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A</span> but a weights’ master-copy is employed</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Line <span id="S6.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C</span> uses the Microscaling data-format with the quantized values stored in the IEEE’s float16, this was done to prove the correctness of the Microscaling implementation</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Line <span id="S6.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">D</span> uses the Microscaling data-format, with the quantized values stored in the E4M3, to store the values of the <span id="S6.I1.i5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">weights</span> and of the <span id="S6.I1.i5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">gradients</span>; whereas the values of the <span id="S6.I1.i5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">activations</span> are stored in a vector of bfloat16. The weights’ master-copy and full-precision probabilities and encodings are employed</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Line <span id="S6.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">E</span> is the same as <span id="S6.I1.i6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">D</span> but all values are stored in the Microscaling data-format, using E4M3 to store the quantized values</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Line <span id="S6.I1.i7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F</span> stores all the values in bfloat16, uses the weights’ mastercopy and computed the matrix multiplication by performing an online compression to Microscaling, using E4M3 to store the quantized weights</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">All figures involving Microscaling were obtained by using a block of size 32.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Configuration <span id="S6.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F</span> is the proposed architecture by Rouhani et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, indeed we proved its correct functioning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">When using E5M2 in place of E4M3 we observe an overall increase in the relative error of around <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+5\%</annotation></semantics></math> for all configuration but configuration <span id="S6.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">E</span> that sees an increase of <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+111.33\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>111.33</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+111.33\%</annotation></semantics></math>, this is most likely due to the loss of fractional precision.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S6.T3" title="TABLE III ‣ VI-B Non-standard 8-bits floating-point formats ‣ VI Evaluation and Results ‣ Microscaling Floating Point Formatsfor Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a> shows the relative differences between the base implementation and the various tested configurations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">VI-B</span> </span><span id="S6.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Non-standard 8-bits floating-point formats</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Until now we used the numerical formats E4M3 and E5M2 to store the quantized elements in Microscaling as they are the formats formalized by the Open Compute Project <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. We saw that they do not provide enough fractional precision to properly represent the activation values of a GPT2 model — at least of quantized one. Now we aim to evaluate whether a custom format can deliver improved performance, to this end, we introduce the <span id="S6.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">E3M4</span> format, a floating-point representation featuring three bits for the exponent and four bits for the significand. This configuration decreases the dynamic range while increasing fractional precision.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let us call the configuration <span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">G</span> the configuration that uses E3M4 to store all quantized Microscaling values, that is weights, activation values and gradients.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We observed a relative error of 75% when compared to the base implementation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE III: </span>Relative errors between various loss curves</figcaption>
<table id="S6.T3.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S6.T3.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
