<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 8: </span>Learning curves for different methods</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS3.4" class="ltx_text">VI-C</span> </span><span id="S6.SS3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Exact accumulator</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Until now, we used a floating-point accumulator to compute the Microscaling-accelerated dot-product. Now we present the learning curve for configuration <span id="S6.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">D</span> and <span id="S6.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F</span> when a fixed-point roundless accumulator is employed. Using E4M3 8-bit floats in the Microscaling representation, we need to employ a 43 bits accumulator.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S6.T4" title="TABLE IV ‣ VI-C Exact accumulator ‣ VI Evaluation and Results ‣ Microscaling Floating Point Formatsfor Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a> shows the relative differences between the base implementation and the various tested configurations, while Figure <a href="#S6.F9" title="Fig. 9 ‣ VI-C Exact accumulator ‣ VI Evaluation and Results ‣ Microscaling Floating Point Formatsfor Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> the loss curves during training.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE IV: </span>Relative errors between various loss curves</figcaption>
